# Supabase Query & RPC Patterns

## No `select('*')` in Production

Always specify needed columns. Over-fetching wastes bandwidth and leaks schema details.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('id, name, slug, corridor_id');

// ❌ BAD
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('*');
```

## No Client-Side Full-Table Filtering

If you need to find a row by a non-PK field, use a DB-side filter or RPC. Never load all rows and `.find()`.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — filter in DB
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('id, name').eq('slug', slug).single();

// ❌ BAD — load all, filter in JS
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('*');
const match = data?.find(n => n.slug === slug);
```

## All List Queries Must Have a LIMIT

If the UI shows a list, the query must have pagination (cursor or offset). Unbounded queries become production incidents at scale.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { data } = await supabase.from('sightings').select('*').order('created_at', { ascending: false }).limit(50);

// ❌ BAD — unbounded
const { data } = await supabase.from('sightings').select('*');
```

## Parameterized Inputs Only

Never interpolate user input into PostgREST filter strings. Use `.eq()`, `.ilike()`, or RPC parameters.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('id, name').ilike('name', `%${query}%`);

// ❌ BAD — string interpolation in filter
const { data } = await supabase.from('nganyas').select('id, name').or(`name.ilike.%${query}%`);
```

## Regenerate Types

`database.types.ts` must be regenerated before every PR. Types must match the live schema.
